n    secret: apiKey,\n  });\n  const payload = parseEmailWebhookPayload(body);\n  if (payload.version !== \"1\") {\n    throw new Error(`Unsupported payload version: ${payload.version}`);\n  }\n  if (!payload.run_id) {\n    throw new Error(\"Missing run_id\");\n  }\n\n  const apiBaseUrl = payload.data?.api_base_url ?? \"https://api.lovable.dev\";\n\n  await sendLovableEmail(\n    {\n      run_id: payload.run_id,\n      to: payload.data?.email ?? \"\",\n      from: { name: \"My App\", address: \"noreply@example.com\" },\n      subject: \"Welcome\",\n      html: \"<p>Hello</p>\",\n      text: \"Hello\",\n      purpose: \"transactional\",\n    },\n    { apiKey, apiBaseUrl },\n  );\n}\n```\n\n### The `from` field\n\n`from` takes either a raw RFC 5322 header string\n(`\"My App <noreply@example.com>\"`) or a parts object\n(`{ name: \"My App\", address: \"noreply@example.com\" }`). Prefer the parts\nform: the SDK (`formatEmailFrom`, also exported) quotes and escapes the\ndisplay name, so brand names containing `,` `:` `;` or non-ASCII characters\ncannot produce an unparseable header the send API would reject.\n\n### Sending without a `run_id` (runtime-triggered emails)\n\nFor transactional emails sent outside a build context (e.g., webhook handlers,\nadmin actions, database triggers), `run_id` is not required. Provide an\n`idempotency_key` with `purpose: \"transactional\"` and the API creates a\ntransactional run automatically:\n\n```ts\nawait sendLovableEmail(\n  {\n    to: \"user@example.com\",\n    from: \"App <noreply@notify.example.com>\",\n    sender_domain: \"notify.example.com\",\n    subject: \"Your request was approved\",\n    html: \"<p>Approved!</p>\",\n    text: \"Approved!\",\n    purpose: \"transactional\",\n    idempotency_key: `approval-${requestId}`,\n    unsubscribe_token: token,\n  },\n  { apiKey },\n);\n```\n\nAll clients take the client options (`{ apiKey, apiBaseUrl?, authHeader? }`)\nas their last argument.\n\n### Cc and bcc\n\n`cc` and `bcc` take arrays of addresses (`\"a@b.com\"` or `\"Name <a@b.com>\"`).\nCc recipients appear in the message's Cc header; bcc recipients receive the\nmessage without appearing in any header. A send is limited to 5 recipients\nacross `to`, `cc`, and `bcc`, and the addresses must be distinct.\n\n```ts\nawait sendLovableEmail(\n  {\n    to: \"user@example.com\",\n    cc: [\"manager@example.com\"],\n    bcc: [\"records@example.com\"],\n    // ... as above\n  },\n  { apiKey },\n);\n```\n\nTwo things to know: a cc/bcc recipient who previously unsubscribed or bounced\nis silently dropped from the send (the primary recipient still gets it), and\nunsubscribing is scoped to the primary `to` recipient — the link in the\nmessage body and the mail client's native unsubscribe button both carry the\nprimary's token, so a cc'd reader using either one unsubscribes the primary\nrecipient.\n\n### Listing email logs\n\n`listEmailLogs` returns recent delivery events for the calling project.\n`since`/`until` are RFC3339 timestamps, clamped server-side to the workspace\nplan's retention window. Results are cursor-paginated (max 100 per page).\n\n```ts\nimport { listEmailLogs } from \"@lovable.dev/email-js\";\n\nconst { data, pagination } = await listEmailLogs(\n  { recipient: \"user@example.com\", event_type: \"delivered\", since: \"2026-06-01T00:00:00Z\", limit: 50 },\n  { apiKey },\n);\n\nif (pagination.next_cursor) {\n  const nextPage = await listEmailLogs({ cursor: pagination.next_cursor }, { apiKey });\n}\n```\n\n### Managing unsubscribes\n\nUnsubscribe state is scoped to the recipient and the sender domain's apex.\nSetting `subscribed: true` lifts a sender-driven unsubscribe; recipients who\nfiled a spam complaint cannot be resubscribed — the API responds 409 and the\nthrown `EmailAPIError` has `code === \"complaint_not_liftable\"`.\n\n```ts\nimport { getEmailUnsubscribe, setEmailUnsubscribe } from \"@lovable.dev/email-js\";\n\nconst state = await getEmailUnsubscribe(\n  { recipient: \"user@example.com\", domain: \"notify.example.com\" },\n  { apiKey },\n);\n\nawait setEmailUnsubscribe(\n  { recipient: \"user@example.com\", domain: \"notify.example.com\", subscribed: false },\n  { apiKey },\n);\n```\n\n### Handling email webhooks\n\n`createEmailWebhookHandler` verifies the `X-Lovable-Signature` on each delivery\n(signed with the project's `LOVABLE_API_KEY`) and dispatches to the handler\nregistered for the event type. Write only handler bodies — verification stays\ninside the package.\n\n```ts\nimport { createEmailWebhookHandler } from \"@lovable.dev/email-js\";\n\nconst handler = createEmailWebhookHandler({\n  apiKey: Deno.env.get(\"LOVABLE_API_KEY\")!,\n  on: {\n    \"email.bounced\": async (event) => {\n      await markEmailUndeliverable(event.data.recipient);\n    },\n    \"email.unsubscribed\": async (event) => {\n      await recordOptOut(event.data.recipient);\n    },\n  },\n});\n\nDeno.serve(handler);\n```\n\nEvent types: `email.bounced`, `email.complaint`, `email.unsubscribed`,\n`email.resubscribed` (defined but not yet emitted). Events without a\nregistered handler are acknowledged with 200. A handler that throws returns\n500 and the delivery is retried (up to 5 attempts in total, with backoff) —\nuse `event.event_id` (also sent as the `X-Lovable-Delivery` header) to\ndeduplicate redeliveries.\n\n### Handling auth email webhooks\n\n`createAuthEmailHandler` is the auth counterpart: it verifies the delivery the\nsame way, then renders and sends the requested auth email (requires the\noptional `react` and `@react-email/render` peer dependencies). The `emails`\nmap is total — all six action types (`signup`, `invite`, `magiclink`,\n`recovery`, `email_change`, `reauthentication`) must be present, so a missing\nhandler is a compile error rather than a silently-unsent password reset.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createAuthEmailHandler } from \"@lovable.dev/email-js\";\n\nconst handler = createAuthEmailHandler({\n  apiKey: process.env.LOVABLE_API_KEY!,\n  from: { name: \"My App\", address: \"noreply@notify.example.com\" }, // sender domain derived from the address\n  replyTo: { name: \"My App Support\", address: \"support@example.com\" },\n  sendUrl: process.env.LOVABLE_SEND_URL,\n  emails: {\n    signup: { subject: \"Confirm your email\", render: (data) => <SignupEmail confirmationUrl={data.url} /> },\n    invite: { subject: \"You've been invited\", render: (data) => <InviteEmail confirmationUrl={data.url} /> },\n    magiclink: { subject: \"Your login link\", render: (data) => <MagicLinkEmail confirmationUrl={data.url} /> },\n    recovery: { subject: \"Reset your password\", render: (data) => <RecoveryEmail confirmationUrl={data.url} /> },\n    email_change: { subject: \"Confirm your new email\", render: (data) => <EmailChangeEmail confirmationUrl={data.url} /> },\n    reauthentication: { subject: \"Your verification code\", render: (data) => <ReauthenticationEmail token={data.token ?? \"\"} /> },\n  },\n});\n```\n\nProjects on Lovable Auth pass `provider: \"lovable_auth\"`. Lovable Auth sends\none-time codes rather than links, so the `emails` map is total over\n`signup_confirmation`, `sign_in_otp` and `recovery_otp`, and `render` receives\n`code`, `expires_at` (ISO 8601) and `locale` (the BCP 47 tag of the language the\nuser saw on the hosted sign-in page, `en` when unknown) instead of `url` and\n`token`. The default provider is `\"lovable_cloud\"`, the handler shown above.\n\n```tsx\nconst handler = createAuthEmailHandler({\n  provider: \"lovable_auth\",\n  apiKey: process.env.LOVABLE_API_KEY!,\n  from: { name: \"My App\", address: \"noreply@notify.example.com\" },\n  sendUrl: process.env.LOVABLE_SEND_URL,\n  emails: {\n    signup_confirmation: { subject: \"Confirm your email\", render: (data) => <CodeEmail code={data.code} expiresAt={data.expires_at} /> },\n    sign_in_otp: { subject: \"Your sign-in code\", render: (data) => <CodeEmail code={data.code} expiresAt={data.expires_at} /> },\n    recovery_otp: { subject: \"Reset your password\", render: (data) => <CodeEmail code={data.code} expiresAt={data.expires_at} /> },\n  },\n});\n```\n\n`replyTo` takes the same string-or-parts form as `from` and sets the Reply-To\nheader, so a user who replies to a confirmation email reaches a monitored\ninbox instead of the noreply sender. Omit it and replies go to `from`.\n\n`render` may be async — look up personalization data first, and fall back\n(`?? \"friend\"`) instead of throwing when it's missing: auth emails are\nload-bearing. A per-type entry can also be a single async function returning\n`{ subject, element }` when subject and body share one lookup.\n\nThe auth webhook is a work request, not a notification, so its failure\nsemantics differ from `createEmailWebhookHandler`: unknown action types and\npayload versions return 400 (a permanent delivery failure recorded on the\nrun) instead of being acked, and a render failure or retryable send failure\nreturns 500 so the delivery is retried — the payload's `run_id` doubles as\nthe send idempotency key, so retries cannot double-send. Permanent send\nrejections (for example an expired run) also return 400 rather than burning\nretries on a deterministic failure. The 400 for an unknown action type carries\n`{ \"error\": \"unsupported_action_type\", \"action_type\": \"<type>\", \"supported_action_types\": [...] }`,\nthe keys of your `emails` map, so the caller can tell a missing template from\nany other rejection.\n","readmeFilename":""}